lle businessman who won a competitive primary rematch in August, plus two minor independent candidates. Copeland has run a serious campaign and raised roughly $870,000, but Van Epps has raised more than twice as much and benefits from about $2.5 million in outside support, with Democratic groups also spending against him.\n\nEvery major race rater calls the district Safe or Solid Republican, and prediction markets price a Van Epps win at roughly 93%. With no public general-election polling, the outlook rests on the district's R+11 lean, incumbency, and the money gap, all of which favor the incumbent by a wide margin.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0399, "margin": -11.34}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0635, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0506, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0512, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0318, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0486, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0413, "margin": -12.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.049, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.0174, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.0197, "margin": -14.0}]}